# Line endings for this project's config files.
#
# shortname.py writes JSON and the shortname map with an explicit "\n" but used
# to open the tmp file in text mode with newline=None, so the bytes that landed
# on disk were whatever the OS translated that to -- CRLF on Windows, LF on
# Linux. Every write site now passes newline="" so no translation happens and
# the code's own "\n" reaches disk unchanged. These rules are the git-side half
# of the same guarantee: a config file checked in from either platform stores
# LF in the repo, so a rewrite from the other one is not a whole-file diff.
#
# The .example.* files are the tracked ones; layout.json and
# data/series_aliases.json are gitignored personal config, listed here so that
# if a fork ever tracks them the rule is already right.

layout.json          text eol=lf
layout.example.json  text eol=lf

data/series_aliases.json          text eol=lf
data/series_aliases.example.json  text eol=lf

subreddit_map.example.json  text eol=lf
manifest.example.json       text eol=lf

# The shortname map is NOT covered here and cannot be: its path is
# user-configured via layout["shortname_file"] and normally points OUTSIDE the
# repo (this user's is an absolute path into the Google-Drive-synced archive
# root), so no gitattributes pattern can reach it. The newline="" in
# save_shortname_entry / undo_shortname_write is what makes that file
# deterministic wherever it happens to live -- the only mechanism available.
# The committed template is in the repo, so it can be pinned:
known_series_names.example.txt  text eol=lf
